1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,499
Four United States Navy sailors confessed

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,460
to the same rape and murder —

3
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:05,579
one after another, facing the same detective,

4
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:07,535
in Norfolk, Virginia.

5
00:00:07,595 --> 00:00:09,135
Signed statements.

6
00:00:09,195 --> 00:00:10,689
Recorded details.

7
00:00:10,749 --> 00:00:13,822
Four separate men, all guilty by their own words.

8
00:00:13,882 --> 00:00:15,283
Here is the problem.

9
00:00:15,343 --> 00:00:16,883
None of them did it.

10
00:00:16,943 --> 00:00:19,323
The DNA said so every single time,

11
00:00:19,383 --> 00:00:20,842
and every time it said so,

12
00:00:20,843 --> 00:00:22,722
the detective across the table did not release

13
00:00:22,723 --> 00:00:23,777
a man.

14
00:00:23,837 --> 00:00:25,981
He went out and found a new one.

15
00:00:26,041 --> 00:00:28,464
This story has five confessions in it.

16
00:00:28,524 --> 00:00:30,157
Four of them were false.

17
00:00:30,217 --> 00:00:31,097
Keep count —

18
00:00:31,157 --> 00:00:33,416
because the man who collected the false ones ends

19
00:00:33,417 --> 00:00:38,087
this story in a federal prison cell of his own.

20
00:00:38,147 --> 00:00:40,663
Her name was Michelle Moore-Bosko.

21
00:00:40,723 --> 00:00:42,783
She was eighteen years old,

22
00:00:42,843 --> 00:00:44,863
newly married to a Navy sailor,

23
00:00:44,923 --> 00:00:47,482
living in an apartment near the Norfolk naval base

24
00:00:47,483 --> 00:00:49,462
while her husband went to sea.

25
00:00:49,522 --> 00:00:52,722
On the night of July 7th, 1997,

26
00:00:52,782 --> 00:00:54,721
someone she knew came to her door —

27
00:00:54,722 --> 00:00:56,502
and by morning she was gone,

28
00:00:56,562 --> 00:00:58,921
and one of the worst false-confession disasters

29
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:01,047
in American history had begun.

30
00:01:01,107 --> 00:01:02,266
Before this is over,

31
00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:04,889
eight men in all will be charged with killing her.

32
00:01:04,890 --> 00:01:06,988
Seven of them will be innocent.

33
00:01:07,048 --> 00:01:08,727
The one guilty man will not be caught

34
00:01:08,728 --> 00:01:10,446
by detectives at all.

35
00:01:10,506 --> 00:01:12,805
He will mail his confession from a prison cell,

36
00:01:12,806 --> 00:01:16,186
in his own handwriting, almost as a joke —

37
00:01:16,246 --> 00:01:17,846
and the astonishing thing,

38
00:01:17,906 --> 00:01:20,206
the thing this film exists to show you,

39
00:01:20,266 --> 00:01:22,845
is what the system did when the truth arrived

40
00:01:22,846 --> 00:01:24,214
in an envelope.

41
00:01:24,274 --> 00:01:25,629
It did nothing.

42
00:01:25,689 --> 00:01:27,972
So keep counting confessions with me.

43
00:01:28,032 --> 00:01:29,971
And keep one eye on the detective,

44
00:01:29,972 --> 00:01:34,416
because his ending is the one nobody saw coming.

45
00:01:34,476 --> 00:01:36,655
Norfolk, Virginia is a Navy town

46
00:01:36,656 --> 00:01:39,512
the way some towns are steel towns or coal towns.

47
00:01:39,513 --> 00:01:41,750
The harbor is gray with warships.

48
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:44,149
Half the young men you meet are sailors,

49
00:01:44,150 --> 00:01:46,049
and half the doors in the apartment blocks near

50
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:48,510
the base belong to Navy families —

51
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:50,670
young couples, new marriages,

52
00:01:50,730 --> 00:01:52,917
husbands gone for weeks at a time.

53
00:01:52,977 --> 00:01:55,497
It is a place built on absences:

54
00:01:55,557 --> 00:01:57,257
you marry at nineteen,

55
00:01:57,317 --> 00:01:59,516
you learn to sleep alone half the year,

56
00:01:59,517 --> 00:02:00,416
and the people across

57
00:02:00,417 --> 00:02:01,976
the hall become your whole world

58
00:02:01,977 --> 00:02:03,396
because they are the only world

59
00:02:03,397 --> 00:02:05,013
that doesn't ship out.

60
00:02:05,073 --> 00:02:06,632
Michelle and Billy Bosko were one

61
00:02:06,633 --> 00:02:07,960
of those couples.

62
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:09,549
He was a teenager himself,

63
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:11,046
out on a week at sea.

64
00:02:11,106 --> 00:02:14,040
She was eighteen, waiting for him to come home.

65
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:15,408
Seven days.

66
00:02:15,468 --> 00:02:17,625
That was the length of the deployment.

67
00:02:17,626 --> 00:02:18,625
That is how fast

68
00:02:18,626 --> 00:02:21,085
a life can be standing there one week and gone

69
00:02:21,086 --> 00:02:22,185
the next.

70
00:02:22,245 --> 00:02:24,105
On July 8th, 1997,

71
00:02:24,165 --> 00:02:26,225
Billy Bosko came home from the sea,

72
00:02:26,285 --> 00:02:27,584
opened his own front door,

73
00:02:27,585 --> 00:02:29,776
and found his wife dead.

74
00:02:29,836 --> 00:02:32,435
I am going to say what happened to Michelle once,

75
00:02:32,436 --> 00:02:33,296
plainly,

76
00:02:33,356 --> 00:02:35,435
because you need it to understand everything

77
00:02:35,436 --> 00:02:36,696
that follows,

78
00:02:36,756 --> 00:02:38,795
and then we are not going to linger on it,

79
00:02:38,796 --> 00:02:40,655
because she was a real young woman

80
00:02:40,656 --> 00:02:42,568
and not a plot device.

81
00:02:42,628 --> 00:02:44,887
She had been raped, and she had been stabbed,

82
00:02:44,888 --> 00:02:46,490
and she had been strangled.

83
00:02:46,550 --> 00:02:47,997
That is the crime.

84
00:02:48,057 --> 00:02:49,696
Now hold on to three quiet facts

85
00:02:49,697 --> 00:02:50,937
about that apartment,

86
00:02:50,997 --> 00:02:52,476
because they are going to matter more

87
00:02:52,477 --> 00:02:53,016
than anything

88
00:02:53,017 --> 00:02:55,356
the police do for the next two years.

89
00:02:55,416 --> 00:02:57,096
The apartment was small.

90
00:02:57,156 --> 00:02:59,476
There was no sign of forced entry —

91
00:02:59,536 --> 00:03:01,390
whoever did this was let in.

92
00:03:01,450 --> 00:03:03,469
And the wounds, a medical examiner would note,

93
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:06,090
were tight, clustered, uniform —

94
00:03:06,150 --> 00:03:08,610
the signature of one hand, one weapon,

95
00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:09,817
one attacker.

96
00:03:09,877 --> 00:03:11,092
One attacker.

97
00:03:11,152 --> 00:03:12,599
Remember that number.

98
00:03:12,659 --> 00:03:14,258
It is the only number in this case

99
00:03:14,259 --> 00:03:16,196
the evidence ever supported.

100
00:03:16,256 --> 00:03:17,675
The police needed a suspect,

101
00:03:17,676 --> 00:03:20,676
and grief moves fast in a small apartment block.

102
00:03:20,736 --> 00:03:22,015
A friend of Michelle's,

103
00:03:22,016 --> 00:03:24,535
answering questions in those first awful hours,

104
00:03:24,536 --> 00:03:26,615
mentioned a neighbor from the building —

105
00:03:26,616 --> 00:03:28,964
a sailor named Danial Williams.

106
00:03:29,024 --> 00:03:30,611
He was twenty-five.

107
00:03:30,671 --> 00:03:32,444
He had no criminal record.

108
00:03:32,504 --> 00:03:34,464
But he had seemed, she said,

109
00:03:34,524 --> 00:03:36,611
a little too interested in Michelle.

110
00:03:36,612 --> 00:03:38,013
That was enough.

111
00:03:38,073 --> 00:03:40,493
Within hours of the body being found,

112
00:03:40,553 --> 00:03:42,292
before the physical evidence had said

113
00:03:42,293 --> 00:03:45,632
a single word, Danial Williams was in a small room

114
00:03:45,633 --> 00:03:47,012
at the Norfolk police department,

115
00:03:47,013 --> 00:03:49,180
and the door was closed.

116
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:51,799
What happened
in that room took roughly eleven hours,

117
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:53,660
and it ran through the night.

118
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:55,539
Understand what an interrogation like

119
00:03:55,540 --> 00:03:56,859
that is actually built to do,

120
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:59,339
because everything in this story flows downhill

121
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:00,322
from it.

122
00:04:00,382 --> 00:04:02,665
It is not built to discover things.

123
00:04:02,725 --> 00:04:04,823
It is built to conclude them.

124
00:04:04,883 --> 00:04:06,841
The room is small on purpose.

125
00:04:06,901 --> 00:04:09,277
The chair is uncomfortable on purpose.

126
00:04:09,337 --> 00:04:12,597
There is no clock you can see, no window,

127
00:04:12,657 --> 00:04:15,637
no sense of whether it's midnight or morning,

128
00:04:15,697 --> 00:04:16,716
and the man across

129
00:04:16,717 --> 00:04:19,717
the table is not asking you what happened —

130
00:04:19,777 --> 00:04:21,816
he is telling you what happened and waiting

131
00:04:21,817 --> 00:04:23,370
for you to agree.

132
00:04:23,430 --> 00:04:26,317
Investigators told Williams they knew he did it.

133
00:04:26,377 --> 00:04:28,289
They told him they had evidence.

134
00:04:28,349 --> 00:04:29,948
They gave him a polygraph in the middle

135
00:04:29,949 --> 00:04:30,488
of the night

136
00:04:30,489 --> 00:04:32,749
and then told him he had failed it —

137
00:04:32,809 --> 00:04:35,349
which was false, and, you should know,

138
00:04:35,409 --> 00:04:37,009
perfectly legal;

139
00:04:37,069 --> 00:04:38,888
American police are allowed to lie to you

140
00:04:38,889 --> 00:04:40,269
about the evidence,

141
00:04:40,329 --> 00:04:42,388
and in this room they used that permission like

142
00:04:42,389 --> 00:04:43,589
a crowbar.

143
00:04:43,649 --> 00:04:44,588
Hour after hour,

144
00:04:44,589 --> 00:04:46,908
the same accusation from new angles.

145
00:04:46,968 --> 00:04:49,623
Deny it, and the denial is proof you're cold.

146
00:04:49,683 --> 00:04:52,338
Cry, and the tears are proof you're guilty.

147
00:04:52,398 --> 00:04:55,471
There is no answer that opens the door except one.

148
00:04:55,531 --> 00:04:57,130
And somewhere in the small hours,

149
00:04:57,131 --> 00:04:59,251
a new instrument came into the room:

150
00:04:59,311 --> 00:05:02,505
a homicide detective named Robert Glenn Ford.

151
00:05:02,565 --> 00:05:03,945
Hold on to that name —

152
00:05:04,005 --> 00:05:05,824
this film has plans for it.

153
00:05:05,884 --> 00:05:08,064
Ford took over the questioning,

154
00:05:08,124 --> 00:05:09,703
and Ford brought the hammer:

155
00:05:09,704 --> 00:05:11,140
the death penalty.

156
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:13,729
Confess, and you live to fight it in court.

157
00:05:13,776 --> 00:05:14,755
Keep denying it,

158
00:05:14,756 --> 00:05:17,081
and Virginia straps you to a table.

159
00:05:17,141 --> 00:05:18,700
Innocence, at hour nine,

160
00:05:18,701 --> 00:05:20,340
stops feeling like protection

161
00:05:20,341 --> 00:05:22,722
and starts feeling like a fatal bet.

162
00:05:22,782 --> 00:05:25,021
A federal judge would one day describe,

163
00:05:25,022 --> 00:05:26,562
in a written opinion,

164
00:05:26,622 --> 00:05:28,701
how statements in this case were taken again

165
00:05:28,702 --> 00:05:30,701
and again until they said what

166
00:05:30,702 --> 00:05:32,635
the police wanted them to say.

167
00:05:32,695 --> 00:05:35,350
But that judgment was nineteen years away.

168
00:05:35,410 --> 00:05:36,089
That night,

169
00:05:36,090 --> 00:05:38,029
there was only an exhausted young sailor

170
00:05:38,030 --> 00:05:40,550
who had never been arrested for anything,

171
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:41,469
a locked room,

172
00:05:41,470 --> 00:05:43,389
and a man with a badge telling him

173
00:05:43,390 --> 00:05:45,929
the only way to see the sun again ran

174
00:05:45,930 --> 00:05:47,585
through the word guilty.

175
00:05:47,645 --> 00:05:50,284
Before dawn on July 9th, Danial Williams confessed

176
00:05:50,285 --> 00:05:52,297
to a murder he did not commit.

177
00:05:52,357 --> 00:05:54,792
He had been in that room through the night.

178
00:05:54,793 --> 00:05:56,713
The murder was barely a day old,

179
00:05:56,773 --> 00:05:58,852
the physical evidence hadn't even been processed

180
00:05:58,853 --> 00:06:00,313
yet, and the case,

181
00:06:00,373 --> 00:06:03,299
as far as Norfolk was concerned, was solved.

182
00:06:03,359 --> 00:06:05,118
And here is the detail that should have ended

183
00:06:05,119 --> 00:06:06,659
the case on the spot:

184
00:06:06,719 --> 00:06:08,057
he got it wrong.

185
00:06:08,117 --> 00:06:08,676
His account

186
00:06:08,677 --> 00:06:10,696
of how Michelle died didn't match how Michelle

187
00:06:10,697 --> 00:06:11,654
died.

188
00:06:11,714 --> 00:06:13,440
The method was wrong.

189
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:15,273
The details were wrong.

190
00:06:15,333 --> 00:06:18,252
So the statement was taken again, and corrected,

191
00:06:18,253 --> 00:06:19,453
and taken again,

192
00:06:19,473 --> 00:06:22,307
until it finally agreed with the crime scene.

193
00:06:22,367 --> 00:06:24,186
Read that back slowly.

194
00:06:24,246 --> 00:06:26,786
The confession did not describe the crime;

195
00:06:26,846 --> 00:06:29,455
the crime was used to repair the confession.

196
00:06:29,515 --> 00:06:31,055
Confession number one.

197
00:06:31,115 --> 00:06:32,516
Mark the wall.

198
00:06:32,576 --> 00:06:34,255
One more thing about Danial Williams,

199
00:06:34,256 --> 00:06:36,555
because this case keeps finding new floors beneath

200
00:06:36,556 --> 00:06:37,646
the floor.

201
00:06:37,706 --> 00:06:39,766
While he sat in a cell that autumn,

202
00:06:39,826 --> 00:06:41,626
charged with capital murder,

203
00:06:41,686 --> 00:06:43,890
his young wife was dying of cancer.

204
00:06:43,950 --> 00:06:45,490
She died that November.

205
00:06:45,550 --> 00:06:46,997
He was not there.

206
00:06:47,057 --> 00:06:51,583
He was awaiting trial for someone else's crime.

207
00:06:51,643 --> 00:06:53,682
If Danial Williams had actually killed Michelle

208
00:06:53,683 --> 00:06:57,143
Moore-Bosko, this is where the story would end —

209
00:06:57,203 --> 00:07:00,567
a confession, a conviction, a closed file.

210
00:07:00,627 --> 00:07:04,304
Instead, in December 1997, the DNA came back.

211
00:07:04,364 --> 00:07:05,904
It was not him.

212
00:07:05,964 --> 00:07:08,604
The biology recovered from the scene —

213
00:07:08,664 --> 00:07:10,723
the kind of evidence that does not get tired

214
00:07:10,724 --> 00:07:13,944
at 3 a.m., does not fear the death penalty,

215
00:07:14,004 --> 00:07:16,604
and cannot be corrected across the table —

216
00:07:16,664 --> 00:07:19,254
excluded Danial Williams completely.

217
00:07:19,314 --> 00:07:20,902
In a working investigation,

218
00:07:20,954 --> 00:07:22,758
that is the fire alarm.

219
00:07:22,818 --> 00:07:24,677
It means the confession you built your case

220
00:07:24,678 --> 00:07:25,937
on is contaminated,

221
00:07:25,938 --> 00:07:28,445
and the killer is still out there.

222
00:07:28,505 --> 00:07:29,284
There is a version

223
00:07:29,285 --> 00:07:31,344
of this story where Norfolk hears that alarm,

224
00:07:31,345 --> 00:07:34,005
and Michelle's actual murderer —

225
00:07:34,065 --> 00:07:36,044
a name you will meet soon enough —

226
00:07:36,045 --> 00:07:38,033
is identified within weeks.

227
00:07:38,093 --> 00:07:39,866
That is not what happened.

228
00:07:39,926 --> 00:07:41,805
What happened is the single most important

229
00:07:41,806 --> 00:07:43,446
sentence in this film:

230
00:07:43,506 --> 00:07:45,965
when the DNA cleared the man who confessed,

231
00:07:45,966 --> 00:07:48,495
the police did not question the confession.

232
00:07:48,539 --> 00:07:50,729
They questioned the DNA's loneliness.

233
00:07:50,789 --> 00:07:52,248
Someone else, they decided,

234
00:07:52,249 --> 00:07:53,949
must have been there too —

235
00:07:54,009 --> 00:07:56,208
someone whose DNA got left behind instead

236
00:07:56,209 --> 00:07:57,391
of Williams'.

237
00:07:57,451 --> 00:07:59,490
And who do you look at when your suspect needs

238
00:07:59,491 --> 00:08:00,802
an accomplice?

239
00:08:00,862 --> 00:08:02,124
His roommate.

240
00:08:02,184 --> 00:08:04,144
Joseph Dick was barely twenty,

241
00:08:04,204 --> 00:08:06,584
by every account a pliable, gentle,

242
00:08:06,644 --> 00:08:08,484
easily-led young man —

243
00:08:08,544 --> 00:08:10,523
the kind of kid who says sorry when you step

244
00:08:10,524 --> 00:08:11,712
on his foot.

245
00:08:11,772 --> 00:08:14,271
In January 1998 he went in to face

246
00:08:14,272 --> 00:08:17,072
the same detective, and met the same machinery:

247
00:08:17,132 --> 00:08:19,371
we know you did it, you failed the tests,

248
00:08:19,372 --> 00:08:21,346
you will die unless you talk.

249
00:08:21,406 --> 00:08:23,705
Navy duty records placed him aboard his ship

250
00:08:23,706 --> 00:08:25,446
when Michelle was killed —

251
00:08:25,506 --> 00:08:28,086
an alibi most defendants would kill for,

252
00:08:28,146 --> 00:08:31,213
stamped and filed by the United States military.

253
00:08:31,273 --> 00:08:32,813
It did not matter.

254
00:08:32,873 --> 00:08:35,249
Nobody in that room wanted it to matter.

255
00:08:35,309 --> 00:08:37,189
After hours of Ford's method,

256
00:08:37,249 --> 00:08:38,188
Joseph Dick confessed

257
00:08:38,189 --> 00:08:40,248
to a crime his own service records said he

258
00:08:40,249 --> 00:08:41,772
could not have committed.

259
00:08:41,832 --> 00:08:44,192
And then he did something even sadder,

260
00:08:44,252 --> 00:08:45,411
something the psychologists

261
00:08:45,412 --> 00:08:47,391
who study false confessions consider

262
00:08:47,392 --> 00:08:49,472
the deepest form of the injury:

263
00:08:49,532 --> 00:08:51,081
he kept confessing.

264
00:08:51,141 --> 00:08:54,121
The story he had been handed grew roots in him.

265
00:08:54,181 --> 00:08:56,301
He repeated it to prosecutors,

266
00:08:56,361 --> 00:08:57,741
repeated it to himself,

267
00:08:57,801 --> 00:09:00,300
and would eventually stand up in court and testify

268
00:09:00,301 --> 00:09:00,880
to it —

269
00:09:00,881 --> 00:09:02,780
against other innocent men,

270
00:09:02,781 --> 00:09:04,680
in words that had been written for him

271
00:09:04,681 --> 00:09:05,845
in that room.

272
00:09:05,905 --> 00:09:07,264
That is what a single night

273
00:09:07,265 --> 00:09:09,501
in that room does to a twenty-year-old.

274
00:09:09,502 --> 00:09:11,878
It doesn't just make him say the thing.

275
00:09:11,938 --> 00:09:13,618
It can make him carry it.

276
00:09:13,678 --> 00:09:15,311
Confession number two.

277
00:09:15,371 --> 00:09:16,679
Mark the wall.

278
00:09:16,739 --> 00:09:19,765
In March 1998, Dick's DNA came back.

279
00:09:19,825 --> 00:09:21,180
Not him either.

280
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:23,240
Now, you might reasonably ask:

281
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:24,659
why keep the confessions at all,

282
00:09:24,660 --> 00:09:26,972
once the science keeps spitting them out?

283
00:09:26,973 --> 00:09:27,792
Here is the ugly,

284
00:09:27,793 --> 00:09:30,603
practical answer every prosecutor knows.

285
00:09:30,663 --> 00:09:33,039
To a jury, a confession is the sun.

286
00:09:33,099 --> 00:09:35,800
It outshines everything placed beside it.

287
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:37,940
Alibis wilt next to it;

288
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,160
even DNA, in 1998, could be talked around —

289
00:09:41,220 --> 00:09:42,779
a defendant who described the crime

290
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:45,992
in his own voice must know something, mustn't he?

291
00:09:46,052 --> 00:09:48,800
A confession is not just evidence of guilt.

292
00:09:48,860 --> 00:09:50,280
It is a finished story,

293
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:52,490
and finished stories win trials.

294
00:09:52,550 --> 00:09:54,749
Which means every man that room broke became

295
00:09:54,750 --> 00:09:56,029
a weapon against the next man,

296
00:09:56,030 --> 00:09:58,589
and the file grew heavier exactly

297
00:09:58,590 --> 00:10:00,313
as it grew more false.

298
00:10:00,373 --> 00:10:01,832
By now the pattern is locking

299
00:10:01,833 --> 00:10:03,910
in where you can watch it work.

300
00:10:03,970 --> 00:10:06,346
Each exclusion should have been an exit.

301
00:10:06,406 --> 00:10:09,293
Each time, the theory simply grew another man.

302
00:10:09,353 --> 00:10:10,952
Two attackers became three,

303
00:10:10,953 --> 00:10:13,912
because two didn't explain the DNA either.

304
00:10:13,972 --> 00:10:16,591
Dick, pressed to explain why his biology wasn't

305
00:10:16,592 --> 00:10:19,472
in that apartment, produced more names —

306
00:10:19,532 --> 00:10:21,632
names of sailors he barely knew,

307
00:10:21,692 --> 00:10:24,031
because the room does not accept "I don't know"

308
00:10:24,032 --> 00:10:25,311
as an answer.

309
00:10:25,371 --> 00:10:27,251
In April 1998

310
00:10:27,311 --> 00:10:29,290
a sailor named Eric Wilson was questioned

311
00:10:29,291 --> 00:10:30,590
for about nine hours

312
00:10:30,591 --> 00:10:33,551
and gave them confession number three;

313
00:10:33,611 --> 00:10:34,899
mark the wall.

314
00:10:34,959 --> 00:10:37,289
In June, his DNA was excluded.

315
00:10:37,349 --> 00:10:38,529
Eight days later,

316
00:10:38,589 --> 00:10:40,768
Derek Tice was arrested down in Florida,

317
00:10:40,769 --> 00:10:42,288
and after his own marathon

318
00:10:42,289 --> 00:10:45,344
in that room he became confession number four.

319
00:10:45,404 --> 00:10:46,666
Mark the wall.

320
00:10:46,726 --> 00:10:48,726
Tice, pushed for still more names,

321
00:10:48,766 --> 00:10:50,077
gave them more names.

322
00:10:50,137 --> 00:10:51,717
Three additional sailors —

323
00:10:51,777 --> 00:10:55,097
Richard Pauley, Geoffrey Farris, John Danser —

324
00:10:55,157 --> 00:10:57,176
were charged with capital murder on nothing

325
00:10:57,177 --> 00:10:59,201
but the words of the coerced.

326
00:10:59,261 --> 00:11:00,461
Sit with that:

327
00:11:00,521 --> 00:11:03,141
no confession of their own, no witness,

328
00:11:03,201 --> 00:11:04,701
no physical trace —

329
00:11:04,761 --> 00:11:06,160
arrested for the death penalty

330
00:11:06,161 --> 00:11:08,260
on sentences other men had been broken

331
00:11:08,261 --> 00:11:09,532
into saying.

332
00:11:09,592 --> 00:11:11,411
None of the three ever confessed,

333
00:11:11,412 --> 00:11:12,771
which by this point almost reads

334
00:11:12,772 --> 00:11:14,632
as a scientific finding:

335
00:11:14,692 --> 00:11:17,011
the confessions tracked the interrogations,

336
00:11:17,012 --> 00:11:18,377
not the crime.

337
00:11:18,437 --> 00:11:20,696
Danser's paperwork put him three hundred miles

338
00:11:20,697 --> 00:11:22,956
away in Pennsylvania on the night —

339
00:11:22,957 --> 00:11:25,776
time cards, bank records, the boring,

340
00:11:25,777 --> 00:11:28,244
beautiful proof of an ordinary alibi.

341
00:11:28,304 --> 00:11:30,775
He was charged with capital murder anyway.

342
00:11:30,833 --> 00:11:32,466
Count it with me.

343
00:11:32,526 --> 00:11:34,806
Seven United States Navy sailors,

344
00:11:34,866 --> 00:11:37,406
charged with one rape and one murder —

345
00:11:37,466 --> 00:11:40,385
a crime the medical examiner's own findings said

346
00:11:40,386 --> 00:11:41,805
was committed by one person,

347
00:11:41,806 --> 00:11:43,925
in an apartment so small

348
00:11:43,926 --> 00:11:46,045
a two-man fight would have wrecked it,

349
00:11:46,046 --> 00:11:48,946
with not one fingerprint, fiber,

350
00:11:49,006 --> 00:11:51,713
or cell of any of the seven anywhere in it.

351
00:11:51,773 --> 00:11:53,952
The state's theory now required seven men

352
00:11:53,953 --> 00:11:56,353
to have taken turns in that tiny room

353
00:11:56,413 --> 00:11:59,573
and to have left, between them, no trace at all —

354
00:11:59,633 --> 00:12:02,293
while somehow the trace of an eighth man,

355
00:12:02,353 --> 00:12:04,573
a man police weren't even looking at,

356
00:12:04,633 --> 00:12:05,899
was everywhere.

357
00:12:05,959 --> 00:12:07,558
A federal judge would eventually hold

358
00:12:07,559 --> 00:12:08,598
this theory up to the light

359
00:12:08,599 --> 00:12:12,019
and describe its absurdity in plain English:

360
00:12:12,079 --> 00:12:15,059
multiple attackers, one shared weapon,

361
00:12:15,119 --> 00:12:17,179
wounds of a single uniform depth,

362
00:12:17,239 --> 00:12:19,658
and none of the supposed seven leaving so much

363
00:12:19,659 --> 00:12:21,292
as a cell behind.

364
00:12:21,352 --> 00:12:23,635
Physics testified in this case.

365
00:12:23,695 --> 00:12:25,746
Nobody called it to the stand.

366
00:12:25,806 --> 00:12:27,346
In February 1999,

367
00:12:27,406 --> 00:12:29,465
the laboratory finished the arithmetic:

368
00:12:29,466 --> 00:12:31,926
all seven charged men, every single one,

369
00:12:31,986 --> 00:12:33,801
excluded by DNA.

370
00:12:33,861 --> 00:12:35,308
Seven charges.

371
00:12:35,368 --> 00:12:36,676
Zero matches.

372
00:12:36,736 --> 00:12:38,787
And still nobody walked free.

373
00:12:38,847 --> 00:12:40,427
The doors stayed shut,

374
00:12:40,487 --> 00:12:42,366
the trials stayed on the calendar,

375
00:12:42,367 --> 00:12:44,826
and the death penalty stayed on the table —

376
00:12:44,827 --> 00:12:46,086
because by then, admitting

377
00:12:46,087 --> 00:12:47,686
the truth would have meant admitting what

378
00:12:47,687 --> 00:12:49,350
had been done in that room.

379
00:12:49,410 --> 00:12:50,904
Keep your tally.

380
00:12:50,964 --> 00:12:54,455
Four confessions on the wall, all of them false.

381
00:12:54,515 --> 00:12:56,397
The fifth confession is coming —

382
00:12:56,415 --> 00:12:59,306
and it does not come from an interrogation room.

383
00:12:59,366 --> 00:13:02,453
It comes in the mail.

384
00:13:02,513 --> 00:13:04,153
In February 1999,

385
00:13:04,213 --> 00:13:05,972
a letter left a Virginia prison cell

386
00:13:05,973 --> 00:13:07,233
in an envelope,

387
00:13:07,293 --> 00:13:09,152
written by an inmate named Omar Ballard

388
00:13:09,153 --> 00:13:11,344
to a woman he knew back in Norfolk.

389
00:13:11,404 --> 00:13:13,683
Ballard was exactly the man police are supposed

390
00:13:13,684 --> 00:13:14,709
to find.

391
00:13:14,769 --> 00:13:16,249
He had known Michelle —

392
00:13:16,309 --> 00:13:18,248
a federal court would later note plainly

393
00:13:18,249 --> 00:13:19,629
that they were friends,

394
00:13:19,689 --> 00:13:22,277
that he had often been inside her apartment.

395
00:13:22,314 --> 00:13:25,665
He was the someone-she-knew her door opened to.

396
00:13:25,725 --> 00:13:27,776
And his violence was not hidden.

397
00:13:27,836 --> 00:13:30,215
Days before Michelle was killed, a woman

398
00:13:30,216 --> 00:13:33,015
at the same apartment complex had been savagely

399
00:13:33,016 --> 00:13:34,438
beaten with a bat.

400
00:13:34,498 --> 00:13:35,137
Days after,

401
00:13:35,138 --> 00:13:37,862
a fourteen-year-old girl was raped a mile away.

402
00:13:37,863 --> 00:13:39,450
Both were Ballard.

403
00:13:39,510 --> 00:13:41,649
The second crime is why he was in prison,

404
00:13:41,650 --> 00:13:43,930
months later, holding a pen.

405
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:45,969
What he wrote, in his own spelling,

406
00:13:45,970 --> 00:13:49,089
in his own hand, is quoted in the federal record,

407
00:13:49,090 --> 00:13:52,190
and I'll read it to you exactly:

408
00:13:52,250 --> 00:13:54,629
"And one last thing you Remember that night i went

409
00:13:54,630 --> 00:13:55,469
to Mommie's house

410
00:13:55,470 --> 00:13:58,309
and the Next morning Michelle got killed guess

411
00:13:58,310 --> 00:14:01,088
who did that, Me HA, HA."

412
00:14:01,148 --> 00:14:02,735
Guess who did that.

413
00:14:02,795 --> 00:14:03,639
Me.

414
00:14:03,699 --> 00:14:05,146
Ha, ha.

415
00:14:05,206 --> 00:14:07,025
The letter reached the police.

416
00:14:07,085 --> 00:14:09,404
And now watch the machine do, instantly,

417
00:14:09,405 --> 00:14:12,111
everything it had refused to do for two years.

418
00:14:12,122 --> 00:14:15,288
Ballard's DNA was tested in March 1999.

419
00:14:15,348 --> 00:14:16,703
It matched.

420
00:14:16,763 --> 00:14:18,903
Not partially, not arguably —

421
00:14:18,963 --> 00:14:21,728
it matched the swabs, the fingernail scrapings,

422
00:14:21,783 --> 00:14:22,808
the blanket.

423
00:14:22,868 --> 00:14:24,147
He remains, to this day,

424
00:14:24,148 --> 00:14:26,327
the only human being whose DNA was ever found

425
00:14:26,328 --> 00:14:27,566
at that scene.

426
00:14:27,626 --> 00:14:29,626
Brought in, he confessed freely —

427
00:14:29,686 --> 00:14:31,446
no eleven hours required —

428
00:14:31,506 --> 00:14:34,186
and his confession needed no repairs —

429
00:14:34,246 --> 00:14:37,166
the details fit the apartment, fit the wounds,

430
00:14:37,226 --> 00:14:38,315
fit the night.

431
00:14:38,375 --> 00:14:40,154
And he said one more thing, twice,

432
00:14:40,155 --> 00:14:42,237
once in each statement he gave.

433
00:14:42,297 --> 00:14:43,698
He did it alone.

434
00:14:43,758 --> 00:14:45,113
No sailors.

435
00:14:45,173 --> 00:14:46,667
No accomplices.

436
00:14:46,727 --> 00:14:47,787
Alone —

437
00:14:47,847 --> 00:14:50,567
exactly what the locked door, the small rooms,

438
00:14:50,627 --> 00:14:52,126
and the uniform wounds had been saying

439
00:14:52,127 --> 00:14:53,701
since the first morning.

440
00:14:53,761 --> 00:14:55,348
The fifth confession.

441
00:14:55,408 --> 00:14:56,995
The only true one.

442
00:14:57,055 --> 00:14:58,173
The wall now reads:

443
00:14:58,175 --> 00:15:00,934
four lies extracted by professionals across hours

444
00:15:00,935 --> 00:15:01,674
of coercion —

445
00:15:01,675 --> 00:15:04,834
and one truth, mailed in, voluntarily,

446
00:15:04,835 --> 00:15:06,211
with a laugh.

447
00:15:06,271 --> 00:15:08,030
So they opened the cell doors and let

448
00:15:08,031 --> 00:15:09,622
the sailors go home.

449
00:15:09,682 --> 00:15:11,129
You know they didn't.

450
00:15:11,189 --> 00:15:13,328
This is the part of the story that separates it

451
00:15:13,329 --> 00:15:16,009
from every ordinary wrongful conviction,

452
00:15:16,069 --> 00:15:17,928
the part a federal judge would later stare

453
00:15:17,929 --> 00:15:20,113
at in something like disbelief.

454
00:15:20,173 --> 00:15:23,173
Presented with the real killer, matched by DNA,

455
00:15:23,233 --> 00:15:25,653
confessing voluntarily and alone,

456
00:15:25,713 --> 00:15:28,447
the prosecution did not drop its theory.

457
00:15:28,507 --> 00:15:29,406
It enlarged it —

458
00:15:29,407 --> 00:15:30,326
again.

459
00:15:30,386 --> 00:15:32,025
Omar Ballard, they now announced,

460
00:15:32,026 --> 00:15:33,830
was simply the eighth man.

461
00:15:33,890 --> 00:15:35,690
The sailors' confessions —

462
00:15:35,750 --> 00:15:38,630
the ones that had been wrong until corrected —

463
00:15:38,690 --> 00:15:40,121
were still good.

464
00:15:40,181 --> 00:15:42,500
The man every shred of physical evidence pointed

465
00:15:42,501 --> 00:15:44,760
to was folded into the story

466
00:15:44,761 --> 00:15:46,401
as one more accomplice,

467
00:15:46,421 --> 00:15:48,380
over his own repeated insistence

468
00:15:48,381 --> 00:15:50,963
that he had acted entirely alone.

469
00:15:51,023 --> 00:15:51,913
Why?

470
00:15:51,973 --> 00:15:54,453
Not because anyone produced new evidence —

471
00:15:54,513 --> 00:15:57,089
there was none, there was never any.

472
00:15:57,149 --> 00:15:59,068
The honest answer is smaller and colder

473
00:15:59,069 --> 00:16:00,689
than a conspiracy:

474
00:16:00,749 --> 00:16:02,009
by 1999,

475
00:16:02,069 --> 00:16:04,448
the case had a momentum that truth couldn't afford

476
00:16:04,449 --> 00:16:05,516
to stop.

477
00:16:05,576 --> 00:16:07,859
One man had already pleaded guilty.

478
00:16:07,919 --> 00:16:09,279
Careers, verdicts,

479
00:16:09,339 --> 00:16:11,398
and a grieving family's certainty were all built

480
00:16:11,399 --> 00:16:13,035
on the confession wall.

481
00:16:13,095 --> 00:16:15,134
Tearing it down meant a prosecutor standing

482
00:16:15,135 --> 00:16:16,995
in front of Norfolk and saying:

483
00:16:17,055 --> 00:16:18,834
we broke four innocent sailors —

484
00:16:18,835 --> 00:16:21,754
and the real killer was still free days

485
00:16:21,755 --> 00:16:24,475
after we had our first confession signed,

486
00:16:24,535 --> 00:16:26,794
free when a fourteen-year-old girl was attacked

487
00:16:26,795 --> 00:16:28,567
a mile from that apartment.

488
00:16:28,627 --> 00:16:31,375
No one said that sentence in 1999.

489
00:16:31,435 --> 00:16:33,354
It would take seventeen more years

490
00:16:33,355 --> 00:16:35,774
and a federal courtroom before anyone

491
00:16:35,775 --> 00:16:37,759
with power said it out loud.

492
00:16:37,819 --> 00:16:39,098
And the cruelest bookkeeping

493
00:16:39,099 --> 00:16:40,759
of all is in the dates,

494
00:16:40,819 --> 00:16:42,471
so look at them with me.

495
00:16:42,531 --> 00:16:44,350
Danial Williams had already pleaded guilty

496
00:16:44,351 --> 00:16:45,531
that January —

497
00:16:45,591 --> 00:16:47,431
a month before the letter —

498
00:16:47,491 --> 00:16:49,790
because his lawyers told him, correctly,

499
00:16:49,791 --> 00:16:52,732
that it was the plea or a probable death sentence.

500
00:16:52,769 --> 00:16:56,167
But Joseph Dick pleaded guilty in April 1999.

501
00:16:56,227 --> 00:16:57,256
April.

502
00:16:57,316 --> 00:16:58,578
After the letter.

503
00:16:58,638 --> 00:17:00,085
After the match.

504
00:17:00,145 --> 00:17:02,144
The Commonwealth accepted a guilty plea for life

505
00:17:02,145 --> 00:17:05,004
in prison from a DNA-excluded man a month

506
00:17:05,005 --> 00:17:07,184
and a half after its own laboratory identified

507
00:17:07,185 --> 00:17:08,512
the real killer.

508
00:17:08,572 --> 00:17:10,611
In May, the charges against Pauley, Farris,

509
00:17:10,612 --> 00:17:12,832
and Danser were quietly dropped —

510
00:17:12,892 --> 00:17:14,431
the theory shrinking back down now

511
00:17:14,432 --> 00:17:16,753
that it had its convictions in hand.

512
00:17:16,813 --> 00:17:20,473
That June, a jury acquitted Eric Wilson of murder

513
00:17:20,533 --> 00:17:22,752
but convicted him of rape on the strength

514
00:17:22,753 --> 00:17:24,753
of his coerced statement;

515
00:17:24,813 --> 00:17:26,666
he got eight and a half years.

516
00:17:26,726 --> 00:17:28,726
Derek Tice, who refused to fold,

517
00:17:28,786 --> 00:17:31,445
was convicted in February 2000 and sentenced

518
00:17:31,446 --> 00:17:33,206
to two life terms;

519
00:17:33,266 --> 00:17:34,805
the verdict was thrown out on appeal,

520
00:17:34,806 --> 00:17:37,445
and Norfolk simply tried him again in 2003

521
00:17:37,446 --> 00:17:39,644
and convicted him again.

522
00:17:39,704 --> 00:17:43,044
And in March 2000, in the same courthouse system,

523
00:17:43,104 --> 00:17:45,223
Omar Ballard pleaded guilty to Michelle's rape

524
00:17:45,224 --> 00:17:47,964
and murder and took two life sentences,

525
00:17:48,024 --> 00:17:50,283
telling the court what he had told everyone:

526
00:17:50,284 --> 00:17:51,740
he acted alone.

527
00:17:51,800 --> 00:17:54,176
Hold both of those in your head at once.

528
00:17:54,236 --> 00:17:56,395
The State of Virginia accepted a guilty plea

529
00:17:56,396 --> 00:17:57,636
from Omar Ballard,

530
00:17:57,696 --> 00:18:00,575
who swore under oath that he had done this alone —

531
00:18:00,576 --> 00:18:02,315
while keeping four other men in prison

532
00:18:02,316 --> 00:18:04,089
for doing it with him.

533
00:18:04,149 --> 00:18:05,468
One more thing belongs here,

534
00:18:05,469 --> 00:18:07,733
and it needs to be said gently.

535
00:18:07,793 --> 00:18:10,321
Michelle's parents believed the confessions.

536
00:18:10,322 --> 00:18:12,022
Of course they did —

537
00:18:12,082 --> 00:18:14,641
four men had said the words, in detail, on paper,

538
00:18:14,642 --> 00:18:16,301
and the people who extracted

539
00:18:16,302 --> 00:18:19,008
those words assured her family they were true.

540
00:18:19,028 --> 00:18:20,907
Her mother and father spent those years,

541
00:18:20,908 --> 00:18:22,148
and many after,

542
00:18:22,208 --> 00:18:24,867
certain the sailors were their daughter's killers,

543
00:18:24,868 --> 00:18:27,395
and fighting to keep them behind bars.

544
00:18:27,455 --> 00:18:29,599
That is not a failing in them.

545
00:18:29,659 --> 00:18:32,779
It is the second cruelty of a false confession:

546
00:18:32,839 --> 00:18:34,919
it doesn't just cage the innocent,

547
00:18:34,979 --> 00:18:36,778
it hands a grieving family a lie

548
00:18:36,779 --> 00:18:38,444
to build their grief on.

549
00:18:38,504 --> 00:18:40,964
Whatever anger you're feeling right now —

550
00:18:41,024 --> 00:18:42,506
aim it at the method.

551
00:18:42,566 --> 00:18:45,421
Never at them.

552
00:18:45,481 --> 00:18:46,650
Years, then.

553
00:18:46,710 --> 00:18:48,409
That's what innocence costs once

554
00:18:48,410 --> 00:18:51,315
a system has decided to protect its own story.

555
00:18:51,375 --> 00:18:54,169
Williams, inside since 1997.

556
00:18:54,229 --> 00:18:56,419
Dick, since 1998.

557
00:18:56,479 --> 00:18:58,344
Tice, convicted twice.

558
00:18:58,404 --> 00:18:59,683
Wilson finished his sentence

559
00:18:59,684 --> 00:19:03,124
and walked out in 2005 into a different cage —

560
00:19:03,184 --> 00:19:06,144
a rape conviction meant the sex-offender registry,

561
00:19:06,204 --> 00:19:07,463
and the registry meant,

562
00:19:07,464 --> 00:19:09,924
among a thousand daily humiliations,

563
00:19:09,984 --> 00:19:12,344
that he could not adopt his own stepson.

564
00:19:12,404 --> 00:19:14,063
Add it up across the four of them

565
00:19:14,064 --> 00:19:17,444
and you get more than forty years of stolen time,

566
00:19:17,504 --> 00:19:18,043
for a crime

567
00:19:18,044 --> 00:19:21,653
the state's own files proved was one other man's.

568
00:19:21,713 --> 00:19:24,993
But out in the world,
the case had begun to rot in public view —

569
00:19:25,053 --> 00:19:28,873
because the file, honestly read, is not ambiguous.

570
00:19:28,933 --> 00:19:30,288
It never was.

571
00:19:30,348 --> 00:19:32,827
Any competent stranger who sat down with it saw

572
00:19:32,828 --> 00:19:34,408
the same three columns:

573
00:19:34,468 --> 00:19:36,127
confessions that contradicted the scene

574
00:19:36,128 --> 00:19:37,768
until they were fixed,

575
00:19:37,828 --> 00:19:39,207
DNA that cleared every sailor

576
00:19:39,208 --> 00:19:41,288
and convicted exactly one man,

577
00:19:41,348 --> 00:19:43,588
and that man on record, under oath,

578
00:19:43,648 --> 00:19:45,170
saying he did it alone.

579
00:19:45,230 --> 00:19:48,089
In 2005, lawyers filed clemency petitions

580
00:19:48,090 --> 00:19:49,696
on the governor's desk.

581
00:19:49,756 --> 00:19:52,175
In 2008 came a book about the case called

582
00:19:52,176 --> 00:19:54,455
The Wrong Guys, co-written by one

583
00:19:54,456 --> 00:19:56,255
of the country's leading false-confession

584
00:19:56,256 --> 00:19:57,336
scholars,

585
00:19:57,396 --> 00:20:00,616
and then something close to unprecedented:

586
00:20:00,676 --> 00:20:01,435
a public letter

587
00:20:01,436 --> 00:20:04,356
from twenty-six retired FBI agents —

588
00:20:04,416 --> 00:20:04,535
men

589
00:20:04,536 --> 00:20:07,195
who had spent entire careers inside interrogation

590
00:20:07,196 --> 00:20:08,395
rooms, men

591
00:20:08,396 --> 00:20:11,396
whose professional religion is the confession —

592
00:20:11,456 --> 00:20:12,635
telling Virginia's governor

593
00:20:12,636 --> 00:20:15,356
the convictions were a tragic mistake,

594
00:20:15,416 --> 00:20:17,215
that the confessions had never been checked

595
00:20:17,216 --> 00:20:18,742
against the evidence.

596
00:20:18,802 --> 00:20:21,921
Ten former Virginia attorneys general joined.

597
00:20:21,981 --> 00:20:23,707
Retired judges.

598
00:20:23,767 --> 00:20:25,400
Former prosecutors.

599
00:20:25,460 --> 00:20:27,599
Even jurors from the original trials —

600
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:28,619
thirteen of them —

601
00:20:28,620 --> 00:20:30,100
saying publicly:

602
00:20:30,160 --> 00:20:32,109
we were not shown the truth.

603
00:20:32,169 --> 00:20:33,168
When the people who built

604
00:20:33,169 --> 00:20:34,468
the machine start writing letters

605
00:20:34,469 --> 00:20:35,869
against the machine,

606
00:20:35,929 --> 00:20:37,368
you are watching something rare,

607
00:20:37,369 --> 00:20:40,118
and you should slow down and look.

608
00:20:40,178 --> 00:20:41,758
In August 2009,

609
00:20:41,818 --> 00:20:43,937
Governor Tim Kaine granted conditional pardons,

610
00:20:43,938 --> 00:20:45,797
and three men walked out of prison

611
00:20:45,798 --> 00:20:48,452
after roughly eleven and twelve years each.

612
00:20:48,512 --> 00:20:50,031
Watch the word conditional, though,

613
00:20:50,032 --> 00:20:53,257
because this is a half-open door, not an ending.

614
00:20:53,317 --> 00:20:54,997
Their convictions stood.

615
00:20:55,057 --> 00:20:56,597
They were felons.

616
00:20:56,657 --> 00:20:59,497
They registered, all of them, as sex offenders —

617
00:20:59,557 --> 00:21:01,137
reporting their addresses,

618
00:21:01,197 --> 00:21:03,217
photographed for the registries,

619
00:21:03,277 --> 00:21:05,256
flagged in every background check,

620
00:21:05,257 --> 00:21:07,256
treated by the paperwork of daily life

621
00:21:07,257 --> 00:21:09,816
as the predators someone else had scripted them

622
00:21:09,817 --> 00:21:10,736
to be.

623
00:21:10,796 --> 00:21:13,358
Try renting an apartment like that.

624
00:21:13,418 --> 00:21:15,738
Try explaining it at a job interview:

625
00:21:15,798 --> 00:21:18,999
yes, capital murder, but conditionally pardoned.

626
00:21:19,059 --> 00:21:20,658
Kaine himself, releasing them,

627
00:21:20,659 --> 00:21:23,559
pointedly declined to call them innocent —

628
00:21:23,619 --> 00:21:24,858
the state's mercy arrived

629
00:21:24,859 --> 00:21:27,519
with its accusation still attached.

630
00:21:27,579 --> 00:21:29,139
In November 2010,

631
00:21:29,199 --> 00:21:31,758
PBS aired a Frontline documentary about the case

632
00:21:31,759 --> 00:21:33,598
that much of America found unwatchable

633
00:21:33,599 --> 00:21:35,279
in the best sense —

634
00:21:35,339 --> 00:21:36,659
and understand this:

635
00:21:36,719 --> 00:21:38,366
when that famous film ended,

636
00:21:38,379 --> 00:21:41,240
the story you are hearing now had no ending yet.

637
00:21:41,300 --> 00:21:42,559
The film could not tell you

638
00:21:42,560 --> 00:21:45,580
the four were innocent,
because no court had said so.

639
00:21:45,640 --> 00:21:47,079
It could not tell you what happened

640
00:21:47,080 --> 00:21:48,200
to the detective,

641
00:21:48,260 --> 00:21:50,419
because that verdict was thirteen days old

642
00:21:50,420 --> 00:21:52,420
and the sentence hadn't come down.

643
00:21:52,442 --> 00:21:53,701
Everything that follows happened

644
00:21:53,702 --> 00:21:55,329
after the credits rolled.

645
00:21:55,389 --> 00:21:56,288
Here is what followed,

646
00:21:56,289 --> 00:21:58,648
in the order the truth finally won.

647
00:21:58,708 --> 00:21:59,848
In 2011,

648
00:21:59,908 --> 00:22:02,267
the federal courts finished with Derek Tice:

649
00:22:02,268 --> 00:22:03,988
his conviction was gone —

650
00:22:04,048 --> 00:22:05,627
his lawyer's failure to challenge

651
00:22:05,628 --> 00:22:08,848
that coerced confession was fatal to the verdict —

652
00:22:08,908 --> 00:22:10,048
and that August,

653
00:22:10,108 --> 00:22:12,973
prosecutors gave up and dropped the charges.

654
00:22:13,033 --> 00:22:14,388
One name clean.

655
00:22:14,448 --> 00:22:16,268
In September 2016,

656
00:22:16,328 --> 00:22:18,347
the remaining two men's case landed in front

657
00:22:18,348 --> 00:22:21,168
of a federal judge named John A. Gibney Jr.

658
00:22:21,228 --> 00:22:24,548
He did what nobody in Norfolk had done in 1997:

659
00:22:24,608 --> 00:22:25,627
he actually held

660
00:22:25,628 --> 00:22:27,767
the confessions up against the evidence,

661
00:22:27,768 --> 00:22:30,187
in a two-day hearing, on the record,

662
00:22:30,188 --> 00:22:32,827
with the whole nineteen-year file open in front

663
00:22:32,828 --> 00:22:33,682
of him.

664
00:22:33,742 --> 00:22:35,821
And then he did something federal judges almost

665
00:22:35,822 --> 00:22:36,882
never do —

666
00:22:36,942 --> 00:22:39,662
because the law only asks them to find errors,

667
00:22:39,722 --> 00:22:41,041
not truth.

668
00:22:41,101 --> 00:22:42,480
He ruled that Danial Williams

669
00:22:42,481 --> 00:22:44,440
and Joseph Dick were not merely entitled

670
00:22:44,441 --> 00:22:45,799
to new trials.

671
00:22:45,859 --> 00:22:47,678
They were actually innocent.

672
00:22:47,738 --> 00:22:49,898
His written words belong on screen:

673
00:22:49,958 --> 00:22:50,717
"By any measure,

674
00:22:50,718 --> 00:22:53,217
the evidence shows the defendants' innocence —

675
00:22:53,218 --> 00:22:55,237
by a preponderance of the evidence,

676
00:22:55,238 --> 00:22:57,378
by clear and convincing evidence,

677
00:22:57,438 --> 00:22:59,437
by evidence beyond a reasonable doubt,

678
00:22:59,438 --> 00:23:01,771
or even by conclusive evidence.

679
00:23:01,831 --> 00:23:04,070
Elsewhere in the opinion he put it more bluntly —

680
00:23:04,071 --> 00:23:06,870
no sane human being, he wrote,

681
00:23:06,871 --> 00:23:08,712
could find them guilty.

682
00:23:08,772 --> 00:23:09,672
That October,

683
00:23:09,732 --> 00:23:11,731
Virginia's own attorney general stopped defending

684
00:23:11,732 --> 00:23:14,492
the convictions, and Gibney erased them.

685
00:23:14,552 --> 00:23:16,072
In March 2017,

686
00:23:16,132 --> 00:23:18,491
Governor Terry McAuliffe granted absolute pardons

687
00:23:18,492 --> 00:23:19,712
to all four men —

688
00:23:19,772 --> 00:23:21,172
including Eric Wilson,

689
00:23:21,232 --> 00:23:23,051
who had no court left to turn to,

690
00:23:23,052 --> 00:23:24,771
because he'd finished his sentence

691
00:23:24,772 --> 00:23:26,495
before the truth caught up.

692
00:23:26,555 --> 00:23:29,194
Twenty years after that first eleven-hour night,

693
00:23:29,195 --> 00:23:31,315
the last conviction dissolved,

694
00:23:31,375 --> 00:23:33,257
the registry entries were wiped,

695
00:23:33,315 --> 00:23:35,074
and the words the record now carries next

696
00:23:35,075 --> 00:23:36,674
to their names are the ones

697
00:23:36,675 --> 00:23:38,355
a judge had already written:

698
00:23:38,415 --> 00:23:39,380
innocent.

699
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:42,234
In 2018, the money followed the words.

700
00:23:42,294 --> 00:23:44,133
The Commonwealth of Virginia paid three

701
00:23:44,134 --> 00:23:45,878
and a half million dollars.

702
00:23:45,938 --> 00:23:46,337
The City

703
00:23:46,338 --> 00:23:49,104
of Norfolk paid four point nine million more.

704
00:23:49,164 --> 00:23:52,523
Roughly eight point four million dollars, total —

705
00:23:52,524 --> 00:23:55,903
which sounds like a great deal of money
until you divide it by four men

706
00:23:55,904 --> 00:23:57,303
and forty-plus stolen years,

707
00:23:57,304 --> 00:24:00,203
and realize it prices an innocent man's year

708
00:24:00,204 --> 00:24:03,336
at roughly the cost of a middling house.

709
00:24:03,396 --> 00:24:06,716
Understand what the money is, and what it isn't:

710
00:24:06,776 --> 00:24:08,535
no one was ever criminally punished

711
00:24:08,536 --> 00:24:10,215
for what was done to these four men

712
00:24:10,216 --> 00:24:12,274
in that interrogation room.

713
00:24:12,334 --> 00:24:14,473
The check is the closest thing to an apology

714
00:24:14,474 --> 00:24:15,814
the record contains —

715
00:24:15,874 --> 00:24:18,565
and even the check took twenty-one years.

716
00:24:18,625 --> 00:24:20,723
And now the last tally mark.

717
00:24:20,783 --> 00:24:22,788
I've kept one thing from you.

718
00:24:22,848 --> 00:24:24,148
Robert Glenn Ford —

719
00:24:24,208 --> 00:24:26,627
the detective in every one of those rooms,

720
00:24:26,628 --> 00:24:29,048
the man the confessions have in common —

721
00:24:29,108 --> 00:24:30,658
had done this before.

722
00:24:30,718 --> 00:24:33,818
In 1990, years before Michelle was killed,

723
00:24:33,878 --> 00:24:36,117
Ford extracted confessions from three teenagers

724
00:24:36,118 --> 00:24:38,342
in a Norfolk robbery-murder.

725
00:24:38,402 --> 00:24:39,757
False ones.

726
00:24:39,817 --> 00:24:41,125
It was known.

727
00:24:41,185 --> 00:24:42,424
He was moved off homicide —

728
00:24:42,425 --> 00:24:45,094
and then, quietly, moved back.

729
00:24:45,154 --> 00:24:46,693
That is who was waiting in the room

730
00:24:46,694 --> 00:24:48,831
when Danial Williams sat down.

731
00:24:48,891 --> 00:24:50,550
Ford was never criminally charged

732
00:24:50,551 --> 00:24:52,846
for what he did to the Norfolk Four.

733
00:24:52,906 --> 00:24:54,065
Understand that plainly,

734
00:24:54,066 --> 00:24:56,946
because this film doesn't sell false comfort:

735
00:24:57,006 --> 00:24:58,425
the system never punished him

736
00:24:58,426 --> 00:25:00,251
for its own catastrophe.

737
00:25:00,311 --> 00:25:01,771
But in 2010 —

738
00:25:01,831 --> 00:25:03,530
while the four were still legally rapists

739
00:25:03,531 --> 00:25:05,071
and murderers,

740
00:25:05,131 --> 00:25:07,731
while Frontline was still editing its film —

741
00:25:07,791 --> 00:25:10,548
federal agents came for Ford on something else.

742
00:25:10,549 --> 00:25:12,089
For years, it turned out,

743
00:25:12,149 --> 00:25:13,868
the veteran detective had been taking cash

744
00:25:13,869 --> 00:25:15,928
from criminal defendants in exchange

745
00:25:15,929 --> 00:25:17,569
for favorable treatment.

746
00:25:17,629 --> 00:25:20,284
Extortion, under color of his badge.

747
00:25:20,344 --> 00:25:22,303
And when the FBI asked him about it,

748
00:25:22,304 --> 00:25:23,504
he lied to them —

749
00:25:23,564 --> 00:25:26,388
a federal crime with its own delicious symmetry,

750
00:25:26,404 --> 00:25:28,664
because Robert Glenn Ford, of all men,

751
00:25:28,724 --> 00:25:30,823
was undone by a false statement made

752
00:25:30,824 --> 00:25:33,030
under pressure in an interview room.

753
00:25:33,090 --> 00:25:35,009
A jury convicted him that October,

754
00:25:35,010 --> 00:25:37,742
less than two weeks
before the Frontline broadcast.

755
00:25:37,802 --> 00:25:39,042
In February 2011,

756
00:25:39,102 --> 00:25:41,862
a federal judge sentenced him to twelve
and a half years —

757
00:25:41,922 --> 00:25:43,981
a longer sentence than Eric Wilson served

758
00:25:43,982 --> 00:25:46,494
for a rape that never involved him.

759
00:25:46,554 --> 00:25:47,733
The man who filled a wall

760
00:25:47,734 --> 00:25:49,073
with false confessions went

761
00:25:49,074 --> 00:25:50,333
into a federal prison cell,

762
00:25:50,334 --> 00:25:53,393
and the United States Navy sailors he put away

763
00:25:53,394 --> 00:25:56,554
went home, pardoned, paid,

764
00:25:56,614 --> 00:25:58,073
and declared by a federal court

765
00:25:58,074 --> 00:25:59,933
to be exactly what they had said they

766
00:25:59,934 --> 00:26:02,594
were at hour one, hour five,

767
00:26:02,654 --> 00:26:05,154
and hour ten of that first long night —

768
00:26:05,214 --> 00:26:09,285
before hour eleven finally broke them.

769
00:26:09,345 --> 00:26:11,861
So count the confessions one last time.

770
00:26:11,921 --> 00:26:13,061
Danial Williams —

771
00:26:13,121 --> 00:26:15,365
false, extracted before dawn.

772
00:26:15,425 --> 00:26:16,425
Joseph Dick —

773
00:26:16,485 --> 00:26:18,730
false, rehearsed until he believed it.

774
00:26:18,790 --> 00:26:19,850
Eric Wilson —

775
00:26:19,910 --> 00:26:21,352
false, nine hours.

776
00:26:21,412 --> 00:26:22,592
Derek Tice —

777
00:26:22,652 --> 00:26:23,331
false,

778
00:26:23,332 --> 00:26:26,064
and he paid for recanting it with two trials.

779
00:26:26,124 --> 00:26:27,284
And Omar Ballard —

780
00:26:27,344 --> 00:26:29,643
true, mailed from a prison cell with a taunt,

781
00:26:29,644 --> 00:26:32,724
matched by every piece of DNA in the file,

782
00:26:32,784 --> 00:26:34,284
sworn to the end:

783
00:26:34,344 --> 00:26:35,373
alone.

784
00:26:35,433 --> 00:26:36,912
Four false confessions took

785
00:26:36,913 --> 00:26:39,249
a marathon night apiece to build.

786
00:26:39,309 --> 00:26:41,889
The one true confession arrived by mail,

787
00:26:41,949 --> 00:26:43,009
free of charge,

788
00:26:43,069 --> 00:26:45,588
and the system that had sweated through all

789
00:26:45,589 --> 00:26:47,368
those nights couldn't bring itself to open

790
00:26:47,369 --> 00:26:48,558
the envelope.

791
00:26:48,618 --> 00:26:50,383
That is the lesson of Norfolk,

792
00:26:50,438 --> 00:26:52,238
and it is not comfortable:

793
00:26:52,298 --> 00:26:54,398
an interrogation room is a factory,

794
00:26:54,458 --> 00:26:57,171
and what it manufactures is agreement.

795
00:26:57,231 --> 00:26:59,430
Get the wrong man inside it for long enough

796
00:26:59,431 --> 00:27:00,910
and he will hand you a murder

797
00:27:00,911 --> 00:27:02,371
in his own voice —

798
00:27:02,431 --> 00:27:05,412
wrong in every detail until you fix it for him.

799
00:27:05,472 --> 00:27:07,848
The DNA never lied in this case.

800
00:27:07,908 --> 00:27:09,681
Not once, not to anyone.

801
00:27:09,741 --> 00:27:11,740
People simply preferred the confessions,

802
00:27:11,741 --> 00:27:14,161
because the confessions were theirs.

803
00:27:14,221 --> 00:27:17,387
Michelle Moore-Bosko would be in her forties now.

804
00:27:17,447 --> 00:27:19,266
Her killer confessed in 1999

805
00:27:19,267 --> 00:27:21,727
and is serving two life sentences.

806
00:27:21,787 --> 00:27:23,747
Her parents grieved twice —

807
00:27:23,807 --> 00:27:24,687
once for her,

808
00:27:24,747 --> 00:27:26,766
and once for the years they were told a lie

809
00:27:26,767 --> 00:27:28,157
about who took her.

810
00:27:28,217 --> 00:27:30,517
Four sailors gave the Navy their youth;

811
00:27:30,577 --> 00:27:32,788
Virginia took the years that followed.

812
00:27:32,789 --> 00:27:35,168
And the detective who called that justice got

813
00:27:35,169 --> 00:27:38,168
to test, from the inside, how it feels

814
00:27:38,169 --> 00:27:39,948
when the government decides what your words

815
00:27:39,949 --> 00:27:41,249
are going to mean.

816
00:27:41,309 --> 00:27:43,329
If you believe the count matters —

817
00:27:43,389 --> 00:27:45,608
that four false confessions should weigh less

818
00:27:45,609 --> 00:27:47,469
than one drop of the truth —

819
00:27:47,529 --> 00:27:50,569
hit like, so the next jury that hears the words

820
00:27:50,629 --> 00:27:53,582
"but he confessed" remembers this story.
